1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,650

2
00:00:05,650 --> 00:00:06,150
Yes.

3
00:00:06,150 --> 00:00:07,900
So making you all see
the speaker not just

4
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:12,500
not part of the plan
but I'm glad to be here.

5
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:15,530
And my name is Vivek Natarajan.

6
00:00:15,530 --> 00:00:20,030
And I am a research scientist
in the Health AI team at Google.

7
00:00:20,030 --> 00:00:21,620
A little bit more about me.

8
00:00:21,620 --> 00:00:24,020
Growing up in India,
my parents always

9
00:00:24,020 --> 00:00:27,830
wanted me to be a doctor, to
be precise a medical doctor.

10
00:00:27,830 --> 00:00:30,230
But unfortunately, I was
probably not good enough

11
00:00:30,230 --> 00:00:32,990
to memorize all of
the biology textbooks

12
00:00:32,990 --> 00:00:34,700
that you had to do
in case you wanted

13
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:37,250
to track the medical
entrance examinations.

14
00:00:37,250 --> 00:00:41,760
So I ended up becoming a
computer scientist instead.

15
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:44,060
But as a great man
once said, "You

16
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:46,340
can connect the dots
looking forward.

17
00:00:46,340 --> 00:00:48,570
You only join them
looking backwards."

18
00:00:48,570 --> 00:00:51,980
So through a rather long
winded path, not too dissimilar

19
00:00:51,980 --> 00:00:55,220
from how we actually
train our neural networks,

20
00:00:55,220 --> 00:00:57,290
I ended up working
in medicine again,

21
00:00:57,290 --> 00:01:01,460
this time armed with this
magical new tool of AI.

22
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:04,250
And I can tell you that my
parents are far more happy

23
00:01:04,250 --> 00:01:05,990
with my life choices right now.

24
00:01:05,990 --> 00:01:09,140

25
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:11,150
But they never truly satisfied.

26
00:01:11,150 --> 00:01:15,290
But digressions aside,
my goal for this job

27
00:01:15,290 --> 00:01:17,840
is to peel back the
curtains and give you

28
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:20,270
a flavor of all the
innovation that is happening

29
00:01:20,270 --> 00:01:22,310
at the intersection
of AI and biomedicine,

30
00:01:22,310 --> 00:01:26,000
and how that is being
catalyzed by transformers

31
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:28,910
and large language
models in particular.

32
00:01:28,910 --> 00:01:31,340
So we will spend the
first few minutes

33
00:01:31,340 --> 00:01:34,280
trying to work up from first
principles, why transformers

34
00:01:34,280 --> 00:01:37,010
and large language
models are a particularly

35
00:01:37,010 --> 00:01:39,890
good fit for biomedical data.

36
00:01:39,890 --> 00:01:43,910
And then we will deep dive
into a few papers covering

37
00:01:43,910 --> 00:01:47,360
a bunch of different biomedical
applications settings.

38
00:01:47,360 --> 00:01:49,460
And finally, I'll
present my views

39
00:01:49,460 --> 00:01:51,920
on how this field is
likely going to evolve

40
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:54,230
in the next few years.

41
00:01:54,230 --> 00:01:57,600
And even though my voice or tone
may not exactly sound that way,

42
00:01:57,600 --> 00:02:01,190
I am incredibly excited
by the possibilities of AI

43
00:02:01,190 --> 00:02:02,795
in biomedicine.

44
00:02:02,795 --> 00:02:04,670
And I think we have an
incredible opportunity

45
00:02:04,670 --> 00:02:07,550
in front of us to advance human
health and human potential.

46
00:02:07,550 --> 00:02:09,350
And my hope at the
end of this talk

47
00:02:09,350 --> 00:02:11,810
is you all will feel the
same way as I do today

48
00:02:11,810 --> 00:02:15,060
and perhaps join me.

49
00:02:15,060 --> 00:02:17,700
So yeah, let's jump
straight in, why

50
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:19,710
transformers in biomedicine?

51
00:02:19,710 --> 00:02:21,600
And sorry I'm going
to pick people

52
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:23,010
who are in person to answer.

53
00:02:23,010 --> 00:02:24,690
So maybe if one of
you could volunteer.

54
00:02:24,690 --> 00:02:28,320

55
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:29,810
Go for it.

56
00:02:29,810 --> 00:02:32,188
Why not?

57
00:02:32,188 --> 00:02:33,260
That's a good answer.

58
00:02:33,260 --> 00:02:36,697

59
00:02:36,697 --> 00:02:38,170
Sure, go for it.

60
00:02:38,170 --> 00:02:41,275
We have a lot of [INAUDIBLE]
data, so [INAUDIBLE]..

61
00:02:41,275 --> 00:02:46,550

62
00:02:46,550 --> 00:02:49,010
Yeah, sure.

63
00:02:49,010 --> 00:02:50,940
Medical doctors are expensive.

64
00:02:50,940 --> 00:02:54,100
And a lot of their job is
just like memorizing the DSM.

65
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:56,640

66
00:02:56,640 --> 00:02:59,104
Yeah, that's an important
application setting.

67
00:02:59,104 --> 00:02:59,930
[INAUDIBLE]

68
00:02:59,930 --> 00:03:02,740

69
00:03:02,740 --> 00:03:04,090
Yeah, great one.

70
00:03:04,090 --> 00:03:06,700
So I think all of you
were on the right track.

71
00:03:06,700 --> 00:03:10,420
And so maybe if you just
look at different kinds

72
00:03:10,420 --> 00:03:12,910
of biomedical data.

73
00:03:12,910 --> 00:03:14,860
For example, what
are clinical notes?

74
00:03:14,860 --> 00:03:17,500
I think it's sequence
of doctor gibberish.

75
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:20,530
OK, I did not say
that but let's just

76
00:03:20,530 --> 00:03:24,528
call a sequence of doctor
speak or doctor notes.

77
00:03:24,528 --> 00:03:27,070
Similarly, if you were to look
at electronic medical records,

78
00:03:27,070 --> 00:03:27,653
what are they?

79
00:03:27,653 --> 00:03:30,280
They're essentially a sequence
of a person's encounters

80
00:03:30,280 --> 00:03:31,435
with the medical system.

81
00:03:31,435 --> 00:03:34,450

82
00:03:34,450 --> 00:03:35,680
What about proteins?

83
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:37,690
Going deeper into
the biological stack.

84
00:03:37,690 --> 00:03:40,300
They are nothing but a sequence
of amino acids linked together

85
00:03:40,300 --> 00:03:42,460
by peptide bonds.

86
00:03:42,460 --> 00:03:44,860
And does anybody
know what this is?

87
00:03:44,860 --> 00:03:48,480

88
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:49,360
Go for it.

89
00:03:49,360 --> 00:03:51,240
I think that's how we
store medical records.

90
00:03:51,240 --> 00:03:54,320

91
00:03:54,320 --> 00:03:55,090
Sorry, again.

92
00:03:55,090 --> 00:03:56,578
Well, looks like chromosomes.

93
00:03:56,578 --> 00:03:58,365
It's like they [INAUDIBLE].

94
00:03:58,365 --> 00:03:59,240
You're getting close.

95
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:01,760

96
00:04:01,760 --> 00:04:02,360
Anyone else?

97
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:05,340

98
00:04:05,340 --> 00:04:08,450
So this is in the Wellcome
Collection in London

99
00:04:08,450 --> 00:04:13,220
and this is actually a printout
of the full human genome.

100
00:04:13,220 --> 00:04:16,519
And note, they did
not cheat over here.

101
00:04:16,519 --> 00:04:17,839
The font is super small.

102
00:04:17,839 --> 00:04:21,140
And as you can see
there's a bunch of ADGCs.

103
00:04:21,140 --> 00:04:24,260
The entire printout
contains I think

104
00:04:24,260 --> 00:04:26,840
over 130 volumes in that shelf.

105
00:04:26,840 --> 00:04:29,090
And each page is
printed on both sides

106
00:04:29,090 --> 00:04:32,900
and it's a 4 point font
with precisely 43,000

107
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:34,680
characters per page.

108
00:04:34,680 --> 00:04:36,440
So that is how big
the human reference

109
00:04:36,440 --> 00:04:41,860
genome is, more than
billions of base pairs.

110
00:04:41,860 --> 00:04:45,220
And so again, the genome
is nothing but a sequence

111
00:04:45,220 --> 00:04:47,000
of nucleotide base pairs.

112
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:49,840
So what we are essentially
seeing over here

113
00:04:49,840 --> 00:04:52,630
is sequences are everywhere
in biomedical data

114
00:04:52,630 --> 00:04:54,340
and what is the
best neural network

115
00:04:54,340 --> 00:04:55,780
architecture for modeling them?

116
00:04:55,780 --> 00:04:59,270

117
00:04:59,270 --> 00:05:01,482
And I guess since you
are all in this course,

118
00:05:01,482 --> 00:05:03,440
I don't have to convince
you that the answer is

119
00:05:03,440 --> 00:05:06,890
transformers.

120
00:05:06,890 --> 00:05:08,750
OK, that's good.

121
00:05:08,750 --> 00:05:12,420
But maybe I'll just offer
a few reasons over here.

122
00:05:12,420 --> 00:05:17,390
Firstly as you can see, the data
itself is multimodal in nature.

123
00:05:17,390 --> 00:05:19,620
And we just saw a few examples.

124
00:05:19,620 --> 00:05:22,070
And as someone pointed
out, transformers

125
00:05:22,070 --> 00:05:24,170
have proven remarkable
at gassing up

126
00:05:24,170 --> 00:05:26,420
pretty much any kind of data.

127
00:05:26,420 --> 00:05:29,000
And we are really seeing
this remarkable convergence

128
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,420
across fields, whether that's
speech, or LP, or vision,

129
00:05:32,420 --> 00:05:33,147
or robotics.

130
00:05:33,147 --> 00:05:35,480
I mean, pretty much everywhere
we are using transformers

131
00:05:35,480 --> 00:05:38,690
and I think biomedicine
is no different.

132
00:05:38,690 --> 00:05:41,000
I think secondly, transformers
are far more effective

133
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:45,660
at modeling complex, long range
interactions over sequences.

134
00:05:45,660 --> 00:05:47,563
And this property
is particularly

135
00:05:47,563 --> 00:05:48,980
important in the
biomedical domain

136
00:05:48,980 --> 00:05:53,250
and we will cover this in
more detail later in the talk.

137
00:05:53,250 --> 00:05:55,490
And finally, as
someone pointed out,

138
00:05:55,490 --> 00:05:57,770
these data sets
can be quite big.

139
00:05:57,770 --> 00:06:00,950
And you can easily get into the
billions of tokens territory.

140
00:06:00,950 --> 00:06:03,350
And this is where transformers
with all the power

141
00:06:03,350 --> 00:06:06,530
lies of operations and the
relative ease of training.

142
00:06:06,530 --> 00:06:08,580
And maybe someone should
try training analysis

143
00:06:08,580 --> 00:06:10,460
on these kind of data sets.

144
00:06:10,460 --> 00:06:12,530
You'll realize that these
are much better suited

145
00:06:12,530 --> 00:06:13,905
for the kind of
data sets that we

146
00:06:13,905 --> 00:06:16,650
have in this domain over here.

147
00:06:16,650 --> 00:06:19,080
So yeah, I think there are
a few more reasons as well

148
00:06:19,080 --> 00:06:21,830
but I think these are the key
ones as to why transformers

149
00:06:21,830 --> 00:06:27,450
are particularly well-suited for
biomedical data sets and does.

150
00:06:27,450 --> 00:06:30,660
Any questions so far.

151
00:06:30,660 --> 00:06:31,490
OK, great.

152
00:06:31,490 --> 00:06:34,320
So now in the next
part of this talk,

153
00:06:34,320 --> 00:06:37,010
we will dive deep into a few
papers applying transformers

154
00:06:37,010 --> 00:06:40,050
to biomedical data.

155
00:06:40,050 --> 00:06:41,810
We'll start with
clinical applications

156
00:06:41,810 --> 00:06:44,540
first, and then go gradually
deeper into the biology

157
00:06:44,540 --> 00:06:48,780
stack, looking at proteins and
genomic applications as well.

158
00:06:48,780 --> 00:06:50,720
And what you will
observe is that

159
00:06:50,720 --> 00:06:54,260
while transformers and large
language models by extension

160
00:06:54,260 --> 00:06:58,130
are a great fit, often
you have to innovate,

161
00:06:58,130 --> 00:07:00,620
not just on the modeling
side but also on the data

162
00:07:00,620 --> 00:07:03,170
and evaluation side to
make these application

163
00:07:03,170 --> 00:07:04,370
scenarios really work.

164
00:07:04,370 --> 00:07:07,320

165
00:07:07,320 --> 00:07:09,620
And so the first paper I
want to talk about over here

166
00:07:09,620 --> 00:07:11,060
is this recent
work from our team

167
00:07:11,060 --> 00:07:15,620
called "Large Language Models
Encode Clinical Knowledge."

168
00:07:15,620 --> 00:07:18,450
The motivation for this work is
actually quite straightforward.

169
00:07:18,450 --> 00:07:21,600
So if you look at medicine,
it is a human endeavor.

170
00:07:21,600 --> 00:07:24,560
And language is at the heart
of it facilitating interactions

171
00:07:24,560 --> 00:07:27,465
between people and those
who provide care for them.

172
00:07:27,465 --> 00:07:29,840
Unfortunately, if you look at
a lot of medical AI systems

173
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:33,110
develop till date, these
are all narrow single tasks

174
00:07:33,110 --> 00:07:36,410
single domain models lacking
interactive and expressive

175
00:07:36,410 --> 00:07:37,910
capabilities.

176
00:07:37,910 --> 00:07:39,680
And as a result,
what has happened

177
00:07:39,680 --> 00:07:42,680
is there is this discordance
between what these models can

178
00:07:42,680 --> 00:07:46,400
do and what is expected
of them by patients,

179
00:07:46,400 --> 00:07:49,340
and care providers, and others.

180
00:07:49,340 --> 00:07:51,260
And this in turn has
I think prevented

181
00:07:51,260 --> 00:07:54,517
broad uptake of medical AI.

182
00:07:54,517 --> 00:07:56,600
And you can see that, for
example you don't really

183
00:07:56,600 --> 00:07:57,975
have AI in many
clinics out there

184
00:07:57,975 --> 00:08:01,500
like helping us with diagnosis
And so on and so forth.

185
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:03,720
But the recent progress
with transformer

186
00:08:03,720 --> 00:08:06,650
based large language models,
it offers us an opportunity

187
00:08:06,650 --> 00:08:10,550
to change all of this and
redesign and rethink medical AI

188
00:08:10,550 --> 00:08:13,850
systems with language at the
heart of it mediating human AI

189
00:08:13,850 --> 00:08:19,710
interactions between doctors,
researchers, and patients.

190
00:08:19,710 --> 00:08:21,425
And I will be honest
if I don't point out

191
00:08:21,425 --> 00:08:23,800
that there has been a large
volume of work in this space,

192
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:26,170
particularly in
the last few years.

193
00:08:26,170 --> 00:08:28,110
There have been various
attempts to train

194
00:08:28,110 --> 00:08:31,020
language models in
the biomedical domain

195
00:08:31,020 --> 00:08:32,669
with models of various
different sizes

196
00:08:32,669 --> 00:08:34,502
on different core pieces
of biomedical data.

197
00:08:34,502 --> 00:08:37,169

198
00:08:37,169 --> 00:08:39,780
And while this is
exciting, the quality bar

199
00:08:39,780 --> 00:08:43,600
for applications in the medical
domain is actually quite high.

200
00:08:43,600 --> 00:08:45,060
And so what is
missing is actually

201
00:08:45,060 --> 00:08:48,900
is that there is actually not
many good evaluation benchmarks

202
00:08:48,900 --> 00:08:50,950
and evaluation protocols
and frameworks.

203
00:08:50,950 --> 00:08:54,000
So we don't have the equivalent
of a big bench in medicine.

204
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:57,670
And hopefully, you guys have
covered big bench before.

205
00:08:57,670 --> 00:08:59,520
And so big bench is
a benchmark where

206
00:08:59,520 --> 00:09:01,020
you can assess large
language models

207
00:09:01,020 --> 00:09:03,360
across a variety of tasks,
domains, and settings

208
00:09:03,360 --> 00:09:07,290
but we don't have an equivalent
of that in the medical domain.

209
00:09:07,290 --> 00:09:10,380
And further, if you look at the
evaluations that are typically

210
00:09:10,380 --> 00:09:14,130
used in these previous
studies, they only

211
00:09:14,130 --> 00:09:17,670
look at objective metrics like
accuracy or natural language

212
00:09:17,670 --> 00:09:19,410
generation metrics
like blue or cider.

213
00:09:19,410 --> 00:09:22,920
But these fail to capture the
nuances of real world use cases

214
00:09:22,920 --> 00:09:24,730
in clinical settings.

215
00:09:24,730 --> 00:09:29,430
So what we essentially need was
a good benchmark, and a task,

216
00:09:29,430 --> 00:09:30,930
and also a good
evaluation framework

217
00:09:30,930 --> 00:09:33,620
for evaluating these models.

218
00:09:33,620 --> 00:09:35,650
And so to address
this unmet need

219
00:09:35,650 --> 00:09:39,082
and assess the potential of
LLMs in medicine, in our team,

220
00:09:39,082 --> 00:09:41,290
we decided to focus on the
medical question answering

221
00:09:41,290 --> 00:09:42,370
task.

222
00:09:42,370 --> 00:09:43,600
Why?

223
00:09:43,600 --> 00:09:45,520
Because answering
medical questions

224
00:09:45,520 --> 00:09:47,360
is actually quite challenging.

225
00:09:47,360 --> 00:09:49,960
It requires reading
comprehension skills, ability

226
00:09:49,960 --> 00:09:52,810
to accurately recall
medical knowledge,

227
00:09:52,810 --> 00:09:55,820
and also manipulate
and reason about it.

228
00:09:55,820 --> 00:09:58,090
And furthermore, the Q&A
task is generally enough

229
00:09:58,090 --> 00:10:00,670
and can subsume a bunch of
different application settings

230
00:10:00,670 --> 00:10:02,920
such as summarization
of clinical notes,

231
00:10:02,920 --> 00:10:05,920
clinical decision
support, and also

232
00:10:05,920 --> 00:10:10,450
primary care triaging of
patient concerns and so on.

233
00:10:10,450 --> 00:10:12,120
So we've identified the task.

234
00:10:12,120 --> 00:10:13,690
The next question
is, what data set?

235
00:10:13,690 --> 00:10:15,690
And so when we look at
the literature over here,

236
00:10:15,690 --> 00:10:18,023
what we saw is that there are
several data sets floating

237
00:10:18,023 --> 00:10:20,520
around assessing model
capabilities in a bunch

238
00:10:20,520 --> 00:10:21,940
of different settings.

239
00:10:21,940 --> 00:10:24,960
So what we decided was we should
probably just unify all of them

240
00:10:24,960 --> 00:10:26,710
and put together
in one benchmark.

241
00:10:26,710 --> 00:10:28,740
And so we did that and
we called it MultiMedQA.

242
00:10:28,740 --> 00:10:30,460
And so if you look
at it, this benchmark

243
00:10:30,460 --> 00:10:32,455
now covers medical
question answering data

244
00:10:32,455 --> 00:10:34,080
sets from a bunch of
different settings

245
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:37,560
such as professional medical
questions like the US

246
00:10:37,560 --> 00:10:39,910
medical license exam
style questions.

247
00:10:39,910 --> 00:10:41,820
It also includes medical
research questions,

248
00:10:41,820 --> 00:10:44,070
those based on PubMed
abstracts and so on,

249
00:10:44,070 --> 00:10:47,820
and also questions from live
users and consumers asking

250
00:10:47,820 --> 00:10:49,830
about medical information.

251
00:10:49,830 --> 00:10:51,080
And also, the setting changes.

252
00:10:51,080 --> 00:10:52,747
It could be closed
domain or open domain

253
00:10:52,747 --> 00:10:54,480
and the model may be
expected to produce

254
00:10:54,480 --> 00:10:56,730
long form answer in one
setting and maybe a short form

255
00:10:56,730 --> 00:10:58,300
answer in another setting.

256
00:10:58,300 --> 00:11:03,682
And finally, we saw
that while the Q&A data

257
00:11:03,682 --> 00:11:05,890
sets, which covered consumer
goods-- yeah, go for it.

258
00:11:05,890 --> 00:11:06,890
I have a quick question.

259
00:11:06,890 --> 00:11:09,572
How do you evaluate
long formats?

260
00:11:09,572 --> 00:11:10,530
I'll come back to this.

261
00:11:10,530 --> 00:11:12,220
OK.

262
00:11:12,220 --> 00:11:13,363
So yeah, very quickly.

263
00:11:13,363 --> 00:11:14,530
Finally, when we looked at--

264
00:11:14,530 --> 00:11:16,210
Sorry, one more thing.

265
00:11:16,210 --> 00:11:18,650
People on screen might not
get your questions in person

266
00:11:18,650 --> 00:11:19,960
so can you repeat questions?

267
00:11:19,960 --> 00:11:21,460
OK, cool.

268
00:11:21,460 --> 00:11:24,430
So the question was how do we
evaluate long form answers?

269
00:11:24,430 --> 00:11:28,250
And I'll come back
to the subject later.

270
00:11:28,250 --> 00:11:29,740
And so very quickly,
when we looked

271
00:11:29,740 --> 00:11:33,712
at the data sets that
actually provided consumer

272
00:11:33,712 --> 00:11:36,170
medical questions, we found
them to be quite small in size.

273
00:11:36,170 --> 00:11:37,640
So we decided to augment them.

274
00:11:37,640 --> 00:11:40,060
And so we went out to Google
and looked at the most

275
00:11:40,060 --> 00:11:42,110
frequently asked consumer
medical questions.

276
00:11:42,110 --> 00:11:45,160
And so we curated a data set and
we added that to the benchmark.

277
00:11:45,160 --> 00:11:48,350
And we call that health
search QA over here.

278
00:11:48,350 --> 00:11:50,200
And so-- yeah, again.

279
00:11:50,200 --> 00:11:54,020
How big is the
composite data set?

280
00:11:54,020 --> 00:11:56,620
I'll come back with the
statistics, I'm sure.

281
00:11:56,620 --> 00:11:58,268
So here are a few examples.

282
00:11:58,268 --> 00:12:00,310
So if you look at the
consumer medical questions,

283
00:12:00,310 --> 00:12:03,313
they are quite short in nature.

284
00:12:03,313 --> 00:12:05,605
And so they come from the
health search QA and the life

285
00:12:05,605 --> 00:12:08,350
QA data sets whereas I think
if you look at the USMLE style

286
00:12:08,350 --> 00:12:10,180
questions, these are
like long vineyards.

287
00:12:10,180 --> 00:12:12,160
And so doctors have to
really, really carefully

288
00:12:12,160 --> 00:12:14,110
read through them and come up
with the right answer, which

289
00:12:14,110 --> 00:12:16,010
often involves a
process of elimination.

290
00:12:16,010 --> 00:12:18,220
So again very, very different
application settings

291
00:12:18,220 --> 00:12:22,420
and so the model has to really,
really adapt and understand

292
00:12:22,420 --> 00:12:26,700
it has to do well in all these
settings across the board.

293
00:12:26,700 --> 00:12:31,050
And life QA is interesting
because the reference answers

294
00:12:31,050 --> 00:12:33,245
over here were actually
provided by librarians

295
00:12:33,245 --> 00:12:34,620
so that's another
good comparison

296
00:12:34,620 --> 00:12:37,140
point for us going ahead.

297
00:12:37,140 --> 00:12:39,440
And so in terms
of statistics, we

298
00:12:39,440 --> 00:12:43,130
had a total of seven data
sets in this benchmark.

299
00:12:43,130 --> 00:12:46,070
As I said, we cover professional
medicine, medical research,

300
00:12:46,070 --> 00:12:47,990
and consumer medical questions.

301
00:12:47,990 --> 00:12:50,780
They're again, of
various different sizes

302
00:12:50,780 --> 00:12:53,660
and can be long form,
short form, open domain,

303
00:12:53,660 --> 00:12:55,350
and close domain,
so very diverse.

304
00:12:55,350 --> 00:12:57,642
And I think it provides a
very comprehensive evaluation

305
00:12:57,642 --> 00:13:00,650
of models in this medical
co-sponsoring setting.

306
00:13:00,650 --> 00:13:04,390

307
00:13:04,390 --> 00:13:06,990
So if you have a task on the
benchmark, the next question,

308
00:13:06,990 --> 00:13:10,530
again, I think I asked was, how
do we evaluate these models?

309
00:13:10,530 --> 00:13:13,290
And as I mentioned
before, automated metrics

310
00:13:13,290 --> 00:13:15,690
are actually deeply
unsatisfactory because they

311
00:13:15,690 --> 00:13:17,880
fail to capture the
nuances of real world

312
00:13:17,880 --> 00:13:19,300
clinical applications.

313
00:13:19,300 --> 00:13:21,780
So what we did was
actually heavily inspired

314
00:13:21,780 --> 00:13:23,310
by some of Stephen's
work over here,

315
00:13:23,310 --> 00:13:25,440
was to put together a
human evaluation framework

316
00:13:25,440 --> 00:13:28,400
for assessing these
long form answers.

317
00:13:28,400 --> 00:13:31,450
And this had two parts.

318
00:13:31,450 --> 00:13:33,810
The first part was
evaluation by clinicians

319
00:13:33,810 --> 00:13:36,870
and we asked them to rate
the model responses along 12

320
00:13:36,870 --> 00:13:40,470
axes pertaining to factuality
of the responses, ability

321
00:13:40,470 --> 00:13:43,440
to recall medical knowledge,
do medical reasoning,

322
00:13:43,440 --> 00:13:45,180
and also for the
potential of harm

323
00:13:45,180 --> 00:13:48,730
and bias in these responses.

324
00:13:48,730 --> 00:13:51,930
But if you look at the potential
end users of such medical Q&A

325
00:13:51,930 --> 00:13:54,520
systems, these are likely going
to be non-expert lay users.

326
00:13:54,520 --> 00:13:57,480
So it is also important to
get these answers evaluated

327
00:13:57,480 --> 00:13:58,300
by them as well.

328
00:13:58,300 --> 00:14:00,570
And so we also
additionally asked

329
00:14:00,570 --> 00:14:03,630
a pool of lay users as to
how helpful and actionable

330
00:14:03,630 --> 00:14:07,180
they thought the answers were.

331
00:14:07,180 --> 00:14:10,440
And so that was our
evaluation framework.

332
00:14:10,440 --> 00:14:13,260
And we also have the
benchmark fixed so now

333
00:14:13,260 --> 00:14:16,050
we monitor the fun part of
building and aligning LLMs

334
00:14:16,050 --> 00:14:18,920
to the medical domain task.

335
00:14:18,920 --> 00:14:21,670
So in this work, we decided
to build on the PaLM

336
00:14:21,670 --> 00:14:23,590
family of language models.

337
00:14:23,590 --> 00:14:25,510
Has that been covered
in the course before?

338
00:14:25,510 --> 00:14:26,770
OK, great.

339
00:14:26,770 --> 00:14:30,250
But very quickly, I believe this
is still the largest publicly

340
00:14:30,250 --> 00:14:33,580
announced densely activated
decoder-only large language

341
00:14:33,580 --> 00:14:37,060
model, with the largest one
being 540 billion parameters

342
00:14:37,060 --> 00:14:39,540
in total.

343
00:14:39,540 --> 00:14:40,500
A few more details.

344
00:14:40,500 --> 00:14:43,620
The model's trained
on 40 billion tokens,

345
00:14:43,620 --> 00:14:46,150
25% of which is multilingual.

346
00:14:46,150 --> 00:14:48,310
The data come from a bunch
of different sources,

347
00:14:48,310 --> 00:14:51,870
including social media
conversations, web pages,

348
00:14:51,870 --> 00:14:55,090
books, GitHub, and Wikipedia,
and so on and so forth.

349
00:14:55,090 --> 00:14:56,760
And at the time of
release, the model

350
00:14:56,760 --> 00:14:59,310
was state of the art on many
NLP reasoning benchmarks.

351
00:14:59,310 --> 00:15:01,500
And also it was the
first model to exceed

352
00:15:01,500 --> 00:15:04,230
the average human
performance on BIG-bench.

353
00:15:04,230 --> 00:15:07,183
Further, over the last
year, PaLM-derived models

354
00:15:07,183 --> 00:15:08,850
were shown to be super
useful in a bunch

355
00:15:08,850 --> 00:15:10,267
of different
application settings,

356
00:15:10,267 --> 00:15:12,500
including for code generation
which was the PaLM code

357
00:15:12,500 --> 00:15:15,090
model, in robotics,
the PaLM second model,

358
00:15:15,090 --> 00:15:17,880
and also for answering math
and science questions which

359
00:15:17,880 --> 00:15:18,928
was the Minerva models.

360
00:15:18,928 --> 00:15:20,970
And so we thought PaLM
was a very good foundation

361
00:15:20,970 --> 00:15:23,345
model for us to build on and
use it in the medical domain

362
00:15:23,345 --> 00:15:24,460
as well.

363
00:15:24,460 --> 00:15:26,830
And overall, I think PaLM is
a true magic of engineering

364
00:15:26,830 --> 00:15:29,940
but I will refer you all
back to [INAUDIBLE] paper

365
00:15:29,940 --> 00:15:31,260
on this for more details.

366
00:15:31,260 --> 00:15:34,660
I think it's a must read.

367
00:15:34,660 --> 00:15:37,120
And again, in late
October last year,

368
00:15:37,120 --> 00:15:39,310
Jason Wei and a few
others at Google Brain

369
00:15:39,310 --> 00:15:42,640
came out with the FLAN PaLM
variant of the PaLM model

370
00:15:42,640 --> 00:15:45,250
and this is basically the
instruction to and counterpart.

371
00:15:45,250 --> 00:15:47,050
And this model was
even better than PaLM

372
00:15:47,050 --> 00:15:49,000
and I believe there's
still the state

373
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,952
of the art on many benchmarks
such as MMLU, [INAUDIBLE],,

374
00:15:51,952 --> 00:15:54,160
and I think it exceeds PaLM
performance by an average

375
00:15:54,160 --> 00:15:56,785
of 9.4% across BIG-bench tasks.

376
00:15:56,785 --> 00:15:59,660

377
00:15:59,660 --> 00:16:01,820
So we decided to build
on the FLAN-PaLM model

378
00:16:01,820 --> 00:16:04,340
and we applied a combination
of prompting strategies

379
00:16:04,340 --> 00:16:06,800
including few short
prompting, chain

380
00:16:06,800 --> 00:16:09,780
of thought reasoning, and also
self consistency to the 540

381
00:16:09,780 --> 00:16:11,000
billion parameter variant.

382
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,310
And we evaluated it
on the MultiMedQA data

383
00:16:13,310 --> 00:16:16,790
sets that had the short
form MCQ questions.

384
00:16:16,790 --> 00:16:19,490
And we found that this
model was really good.

385
00:16:19,490 --> 00:16:23,390
At the time of publication,
this model on the USMLE data set

386
00:16:23,390 --> 00:16:26,850
exceeded the previous state
of the art by over 17%.

387
00:16:26,850 --> 00:16:29,450
This is specifically
the short form value?

388
00:16:29,450 --> 00:16:31,930
It's only for the
USMLE MedQA data set.

389
00:16:31,930 --> 00:16:32,748
[INAUDIBLE]

390
00:16:32,748 --> 00:16:33,290
That's right.

391
00:16:33,290 --> 00:16:34,160
OK.

392
00:16:34,160 --> 00:16:36,410
And so you see that the
accuracy or the previous state

393
00:16:36,410 --> 00:16:37,952
of the art at the
time of publication

394
00:16:37,952 --> 00:16:39,590
went up by over 17%.

395
00:16:39,590 --> 00:16:43,340
And I believe this was the first
LLM based AI system to obtain

396
00:16:43,340 --> 00:16:45,620
a passing equivalent
score which was 60%

397
00:16:45,620 --> 00:16:49,180
or above on this benchmark.

398
00:16:49,180 --> 00:16:51,550
And similarly, when we
looked at other MCQA data

399
00:16:51,550 --> 00:16:52,640
sets in the benchmark.

400
00:16:52,640 --> 00:16:54,832
For example, MedMCQA
which is a data

401
00:16:54,832 --> 00:16:57,040
set of Indian medical entrance
examination questions,

402
00:16:57,040 --> 00:16:58,780
the model was again
the state of the art.

403
00:16:58,780 --> 00:17:00,880
On PubMedQA which was
question answering based

404
00:17:00,880 --> 00:17:02,830
on PubMed abstracts,
that was again,

405
00:17:02,830 --> 00:17:05,530
the model was state of the art
at the time of publication.

406
00:17:05,530 --> 00:17:08,200
And same story on
MMLU clinical topics

407
00:17:08,200 --> 00:17:12,694
as well, which include genetics,
anatomy, professional medicine,

408
00:17:12,694 --> 00:17:15,069
clinical knowledge, and a
bunch of other topics in there.

409
00:17:15,069 --> 00:17:19,088

410
00:17:19,088 --> 00:17:19,880
All this was great.

411
00:17:19,880 --> 00:17:22,297
And then when we started looking
at the scaling plots what

412
00:17:22,297 --> 00:17:24,069
we saw was that the
performance seemed

413
00:17:24,069 --> 00:17:28,580
to be improving as we scaled
the model from 8 billion

414
00:17:28,580 --> 00:17:30,820
to 62 billion to 540 billion.

415
00:17:30,820 --> 00:17:32,380
And so what this
basically suggested

416
00:17:32,380 --> 00:17:34,720
that these general purpose
large language models trained

417
00:17:34,720 --> 00:17:37,210
on public internet seemed
to encode clinical knowledge

418
00:17:37,210 --> 00:17:40,450
pretty well, and their
medical reasoning abilities

419
00:17:40,450 --> 00:17:44,467
tend to scale with
model parameter size.

420
00:17:44,467 --> 00:17:46,050
We also did another
experiment when we

421
00:17:46,050 --> 00:17:48,060
looked at selective prediction.

422
00:17:48,060 --> 00:17:51,450
And we used the self
consistency votes

423
00:17:51,450 --> 00:17:52,770
to determine when to differ.

424
00:17:52,770 --> 00:17:55,530
And this is important
in clinical settings

425
00:17:55,530 --> 00:17:58,455
because doctors
communicate when they

426
00:17:58,455 --> 00:17:59,580
don't know about something.

427
00:17:59,580 --> 00:18:00,870
And if our AI systems
are going to be

428
00:18:00,870 --> 00:18:03,078
used in clinical settings,
for example for diagnosis,

429
00:18:03,078 --> 00:18:05,920
they should be able to tell you
when they don't know something.

430
00:18:05,920 --> 00:18:09,870
And so what we observed here
was this fairly crude metric.

431
00:18:09,870 --> 00:18:12,120
We were getting a linear
improvement in performance

432
00:18:12,120 --> 00:18:15,330
as we change the
default threshold.

433
00:18:15,330 --> 00:18:17,550
And this was quite nice
but in practice, this

434
00:18:17,550 --> 00:18:19,890
is actually quite inefficient
because you are generating

435
00:18:19,890 --> 00:18:22,740
multiple decoding samples to
be able to compute this metric

436
00:18:22,740 --> 00:18:23,880
so we need a better method.

437
00:18:23,880 --> 00:18:26,580
Just to be clear, what is the
current fraction [INAUDIBLE]

438
00:18:26,580 --> 00:18:29,195
what fraction you
find the model asks?

439
00:18:29,195 --> 00:18:31,320
Yeah, it's basically says
I'm uncertain or unknown.

440
00:18:31,320 --> 00:18:35,470
And that's determined based
on the cell consistency works.

441
00:18:35,470 --> 00:18:35,970
I see.

442
00:18:35,970 --> 00:18:36,300
OK.

443
00:18:36,300 --> 00:18:38,940
So [INAUDIBLE] to apply variance
and apply consistency samples.

444
00:18:38,940 --> 00:18:40,490
Got it.

445
00:18:40,490 --> 00:18:42,390
Exactly.

446
00:18:42,390 --> 00:18:43,630
Oh.

447
00:18:43,630 --> 00:18:46,420
Are models trained to be
capable of deferring itself

448
00:18:46,420 --> 00:18:49,880
[INAUDIBLE] I don't know?

449
00:18:49,880 --> 00:18:52,380
No because they're just trained
on this next word prediction

450
00:18:52,380 --> 00:18:53,963
task and that depends
on the data set.

451
00:18:53,963 --> 00:18:56,037
The PubMedQA has some
answers which are maybe

452
00:18:56,037 --> 00:18:58,620
but again, we don't explicitly
fine tune the models over here.

453
00:18:58,620 --> 00:19:01,490
So no, the models
are not trained.

454
00:19:01,490 --> 00:19:01,990
Yeah.

455
00:19:01,990 --> 00:19:03,600
So [INAUDIBLE].

456
00:19:03,600 --> 00:19:17,290

457
00:19:17,290 --> 00:19:17,790
No.

458
00:19:17,790 --> 00:19:20,318
So this is primarily
based on the reference

459
00:19:20,318 --> 00:19:22,860
in the data sets, which is-- so
this is all accuracy metrics.

460
00:19:22,860 --> 00:19:25,350
So we already know between the
four options or five options

461
00:19:25,350 --> 00:19:26,440
which one is the right one.

462
00:19:26,440 --> 00:19:27,840
And so we just do
the classification.

463
00:19:27,840 --> 00:19:28,350
Yeah.

464
00:19:28,350 --> 00:19:30,475
So I'll come back to the
clinician evaluation a bit

465
00:19:30,475 --> 00:19:31,097
later.

466
00:19:31,097 --> 00:19:32,430
Sorry, maybe I missed something.

467
00:19:32,430 --> 00:19:35,170
How do you measure
the uncertainty?

468
00:19:35,170 --> 00:19:38,370
So if you know what self
consistency prompting, what

469
00:19:38,370 --> 00:19:41,760
we do is we generate multiple
decodes from the same model

470
00:19:41,760 --> 00:19:45,480
and then we see
the number of times

471
00:19:45,480 --> 00:19:47,315
the highest ranking
answer is worded.

472
00:19:47,315 --> 00:19:49,440
And based on that, you can
fix a threshold and say,

473
00:19:49,440 --> 00:19:51,880
"If it's below this number,
I'm going to differ."

474
00:19:51,880 --> 00:19:54,810
So if say the majority
answer comes up in your self

475
00:19:54,810 --> 00:19:58,840
consistency decode only n
times out of k or whatever,

476
00:19:58,840 --> 00:20:00,845
then if that n is
too small, then it's

477
00:20:00,845 --> 00:20:02,220
very likely the
model's uncertain

478
00:20:02,220 --> 00:20:03,220
so that's how we differ.

479
00:20:03,220 --> 00:20:09,556

480
00:20:09,556 --> 00:20:13,420
So you don't really see
a paper on the spot,

481
00:20:13,420 --> 00:20:17,950
so it's natural that that's
where the rest of [INAUDIBLE]??

482
00:20:17,950 --> 00:20:21,070
I think if you plot it
further, it'll flatline.

483
00:20:21,070 --> 00:20:22,340
But again, that's not useful.

484
00:20:22,340 --> 00:20:23,560
I mean, if you're saying
no to every question,

485
00:20:23,560 --> 00:20:25,060
that's not useful
at all so you want

486
00:20:25,060 --> 00:20:27,185
to have a reasonable definite
percentage over here.

487
00:20:27,185 --> 00:20:29,680
Yeah, but 0.4 or 0.5 not that--

488
00:20:29,680 --> 00:20:31,750
I think that's high.

489
00:20:31,750 --> 00:20:34,030
I think that's still
high, 50% is quite high.

490
00:20:34,030 --> 00:20:36,497
But again, this is a
very contrived setting.

491
00:20:36,497 --> 00:20:38,080
But in real world
use case is probably

492
00:20:38,080 --> 00:20:39,788
I think that number
should be much lower.

493
00:20:39,788 --> 00:20:42,970

494
00:20:42,970 --> 00:20:43,760
[INAUDIBLE]

495
00:20:43,760 --> 00:20:59,388

496
00:20:59,388 --> 00:20:59,930
That's right.

497
00:20:59,930 --> 00:21:01,820
I think balanced accuracy
might be a better metric

498
00:21:01,820 --> 00:21:03,528
but we looked at some
of these data sets.

499
00:21:03,528 --> 00:21:06,260
And one data set, the
skew was pretty bad,

500
00:21:06,260 --> 00:21:07,190
the PubMedQA data set.

501
00:21:07,190 --> 00:21:08,565
And I think no
one should use it.

502
00:21:08,565 --> 00:21:10,980
So if anyone's reporting
numbers on that data set,

503
00:21:10,980 --> 00:21:12,710
you should just distrust them.

504
00:21:12,710 --> 00:21:15,000
And I'm talking about
very specific people.

505
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:18,170
But again, I think
as I mentioned,

506
00:21:18,170 --> 00:21:20,762
these accuracy metrics are good
for publicity and pushing up

507
00:21:20,762 --> 00:21:22,470
benchmark numbers and
so on and so forth.

508
00:21:22,470 --> 00:21:24,770
But the real evaluation is human
evaluation of the long form

509
00:21:24,770 --> 00:21:27,020
answers and that's what I'll
come to in the next part.

510
00:21:27,020 --> 00:21:31,490

511
00:21:31,490 --> 00:21:32,750
So far so good.

512
00:21:32,750 --> 00:21:36,182
I mean, we were getting
results on these benchmarks

513
00:21:36,182 --> 00:21:37,140
and we were very happy.

514
00:21:37,140 --> 00:21:38,385
And so what we did was--

515
00:21:38,385 --> 00:21:40,760
one thing you'll observe that
I have so far only reported

516
00:21:40,760 --> 00:21:44,220
results on multiple choice
questions, short form answers.

517
00:21:44,220 --> 00:21:46,880
So what was left for us to do
was to take these answers--

518
00:21:46,880 --> 00:21:48,880
take these models and
generate long term answers

519
00:21:48,880 --> 00:21:50,390
to the other data
sets that we had

520
00:21:50,390 --> 00:21:52,020
and get them human evaluated.

521
00:21:52,020 --> 00:21:56,150
And I think that is where
the real project began.

522
00:21:56,150 --> 00:22:02,780
When we looked at the evals
by experts and laypeople,

523
00:22:02,780 --> 00:22:05,780
it revealed very key
gaps and limitations

524
00:22:05,780 --> 00:22:07,388
in the FLAN-PaLM responses.

525
00:22:07,388 --> 00:22:08,930
We are often seeing
that these models

526
00:22:08,930 --> 00:22:11,570
were hallucinating or
producing incomplete responses.

527
00:22:11,570 --> 00:22:14,278
And when we asked experts
whether they preferred

528
00:22:14,278 --> 00:22:16,820
clinician generated answers or
these model generated answers,

529
00:22:16,820 --> 00:22:21,260
they almost always preferred
clinician generated answers.

530
00:22:21,260 --> 00:22:23,572
So it was very clear that--

531
00:22:23,572 --> 00:22:25,030
Sorry, I didn't
catch this earlier,

532
00:22:25,030 --> 00:22:26,870
but [INAUDIBLE] evaluators.

533
00:22:26,870 --> 00:22:29,210
Are these laypeople or these--

534
00:22:29,210 --> 00:22:29,930
They're both.

535
00:22:29,930 --> 00:22:32,250
OK.

536
00:22:32,250 --> 00:22:34,130
And so what these
previous results

537
00:22:34,130 --> 00:22:36,530
showed was while
these models already

538
00:22:36,530 --> 00:22:38,630
encode some degree of
clinical knowledge,

539
00:22:38,630 --> 00:22:40,688
to be really used in
actual real world settings,

540
00:22:40,688 --> 00:22:42,230
you need to align
these models better

541
00:22:42,230 --> 00:22:43,700
to the safety
critical requirements

542
00:22:43,700 --> 00:22:45,410
of the medical domain.

543
00:22:45,410 --> 00:22:47,420
But a big challenge
is we did not

544
00:22:47,420 --> 00:22:49,310
have any kind of
supervised or feedback data

545
00:22:49,310 --> 00:22:51,200
and so we really need
the alignment technique

546
00:22:51,200 --> 00:22:53,630
to be data efficient.

547
00:22:53,630 --> 00:22:55,610
But thankfully,
we had instruction

548
00:22:55,610 --> 00:22:58,755
from tuning which was
introduced by Brian

549
00:22:58,755 --> 00:23:01,130
Lester and a few others at
Google a couple of years back.

550
00:23:01,130 --> 00:23:05,570
And how this method
works is it essentially

551
00:23:05,570 --> 00:23:08,810
freezes the big LLM
model and only learns

552
00:23:08,810 --> 00:23:12,452
an additional small
set of prompt vectors

553
00:23:12,452 --> 00:23:13,910
which can then be
used to condition

554
00:23:13,910 --> 00:23:17,090
the model that inference
when doing the generation.

555
00:23:17,090 --> 00:23:18,650
And the nice thing
about this is it

556
00:23:18,650 --> 00:23:23,660
allows very easy use of the
model across tasks and domains.

557
00:23:23,660 --> 00:23:26,900
And you only need to carry
around these additional prompt

558
00:23:26,900 --> 00:23:28,370
parameters.

559
00:23:28,370 --> 00:23:29,780
And these tend to
be much smaller

560
00:23:29,780 --> 00:23:34,310
than the billions of parameters
that you have in the LLM.

561
00:23:34,310 --> 00:23:37,010
And the other good thing is
this is very computationally

562
00:23:37,010 --> 00:23:37,770
efficient as well.

563
00:23:37,770 --> 00:23:39,560
So if you were to do
enter in fine tuning,

564
00:23:39,560 --> 00:23:41,835
often in our compute
infrastructure,

565
00:23:41,835 --> 00:23:43,460
even with like a few
thousand examples,

566
00:23:43,460 --> 00:23:44,793
that would take like a few days.

567
00:23:44,793 --> 00:23:48,122
Whereas with instruction
from tuning, given

568
00:23:48,122 --> 00:23:49,580
the data set size
has also reduced,

569
00:23:49,580 --> 00:23:51,890
the number of examples that
you need is quite small.

570
00:23:51,890 --> 00:23:54,885
And B, you're just updating
the prompt token vectors.

571
00:23:54,885 --> 00:23:56,510
It meant that we were
able to get model

572
00:23:56,510 --> 00:23:57,810
updates in like a few hours.

573
00:23:57,810 --> 00:24:00,590
And so that was really fast and
enabled really quick iterations

574
00:24:00,590 --> 00:24:02,850
for us.

575
00:24:02,850 --> 00:24:06,440
So this was how we put together
the final Med-PaLM model.

576
00:24:06,440 --> 00:24:08,090
We-- sorry, go ahead.

577
00:24:08,090 --> 00:24:09,050
Sorry.

578
00:24:09,050 --> 00:24:10,160
I'll let you finish.

579
00:24:10,160 --> 00:24:11,240
[INAUDIBLE]

580
00:24:11,240 --> 00:24:12,200
OK, cool.

581
00:24:12,200 --> 00:24:14,790
So this was how we put together
the final Med-PaLM model.

582
00:24:14,790 --> 00:24:16,970
So we used instructions
and exemplars

583
00:24:16,970 --> 00:24:19,280
from a panel of
expert clinicians.

584
00:24:19,280 --> 00:24:22,270
And these are in the order
of hundreds, not thousands

585
00:24:22,270 --> 00:24:24,770
and tens of thousands and you
see a few examples over there.

586
00:24:24,770 --> 00:24:27,110
There's an instruction,
followed by a model answer,

587
00:24:27,110 --> 00:24:28,370
followed by an explanation.

588
00:24:28,370 --> 00:24:31,050
And we use that to learn
the prompt practice.

589
00:24:31,050 --> 00:24:35,390
And so the final Med-PaLM model
is basically all of FLAN-PaLM

590
00:24:35,390 --> 00:24:38,012
plus these additional
prompt vector parameters

591
00:24:38,012 --> 00:24:40,220
which are used to align the
model to the requirements

592
00:24:40,220 --> 00:24:41,120
of the medical domain.

593
00:24:41,120 --> 00:24:43,537
And why this works well is
because as we have seen before,

594
00:24:43,537 --> 00:24:45,830
the model already has medical
knowledge encoded in it.

595
00:24:45,830 --> 00:24:47,318
All we need is to
teach the model

596
00:24:47,318 --> 00:24:49,610
how to use it properly in
the given application setting

597
00:24:49,610 --> 00:24:53,730
and that's what these
parameters do for us.

598
00:24:53,730 --> 00:24:56,390
So the question I wanted
to ask is, nowadays

599
00:24:56,390 --> 00:24:58,385
