00:25:02,880 --> 00:25:08,000
you guys explain how you guys
define a reward or preference

603
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:11,190
model and it was not
confined to [INAUDIBLE]..

604
00:25:11,190 --> 00:25:11,690
Yeah.

605
00:25:11,690 --> 00:25:13,898
I think you can think about
different stages of model

606
00:25:13,898 --> 00:25:14,420
development.

607
00:25:14,420 --> 00:25:17,880
So this is pre-deployment and
release in the real world.

608
00:25:17,880 --> 00:25:20,490
So you can't put a crappy model
out there in the real world.

609
00:25:20,490 --> 00:25:23,360
So even before doing that,
if you can get like maybe 100

610
00:25:23,360 --> 00:25:25,730
examples from whatever exploits
that you can get hold of

611
00:25:25,730 --> 00:25:27,320
and use that to prompt you
in your model, that's better.

612
00:25:27,320 --> 00:25:28,805
That's a much better
starting point

613
00:25:28,805 --> 00:25:30,680
before you expose the
model to the real world

614
00:25:30,680 --> 00:25:33,000
and collect preferences
from real users at scale.

615
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:37,280
And so I think RLHF is also much
less sample efficient compared

616
00:25:37,280 --> 00:25:39,260
to the instruction
from tuning, again,

617
00:25:39,260 --> 00:25:42,460
because you're probably trying
to update your entire model as

618
00:25:42,460 --> 00:25:42,960
well.

619
00:25:42,960 --> 00:25:44,610
So I think this is a
very good starting point.

620
00:25:44,610 --> 00:25:46,400
And so they can both
be combined depending

621
00:25:46,400 --> 00:25:49,130
on how depending on the
lifecycle of the model.

