But even when you're training
on say one private corpus

1021
00:42:23,720 --> 00:42:26,410
and then you're using it in
another application setting,

1022
00:42:26,410 --> 00:42:28,160
you want to ensure
that the model does not

1023
00:42:28,160 --> 00:42:31,002
leak out any kind of PHI
information during a situation.

1024
00:42:31,002 --> 00:42:32,960
So I think those sort of
studies are necessary.

1025
00:42:32,960 --> 00:42:35,200
We haven't got into them yet.

1026
00:42:35,200 --> 00:42:36,150
[INAUDIBLE]

1027
00:42:36,150 --> 00:42:47,680

1028
00:42:47,680 --> 00:42:49,530
So the question is,
what are the next steps

1029
00:42:49,530 --> 00:42:52,440
in terms of improving
these models further?

1030
00:42:52,440 --> 00:42:55,020
Yeah, retrieval is a
very important one.

1031
00:42:55,020 --> 00:42:56,970
Being able to cite
sources and especially

1032
00:42:56,970 --> 00:42:59,832
take in authoritative
sources and use that

1033
00:42:59,832 --> 00:43:02,040
in generating the answers
and also communicating that

1034
00:43:02,040 --> 00:43:03,540
to the users is very important.

1035
00:43:03,540 --> 00:43:07,510
I think how you communicate
uncertainty is very important.

1036
00:43:07,510 --> 00:43:10,750
So we've gotten to some extent
using instruction from tuning,

1037
00:43:10,750 --> 00:43:13,300
but I think that can
be much, much better.

1038
00:43:13,300 --> 00:43:16,677
So I think that's
another big bucket.

1039
00:43:16,677 --> 00:43:18,510
Again, I would stress
on the evaluation side

1040
00:43:18,510 --> 00:43:21,120
like looking at more data
sets, which for example may

1041
00:43:21,120 --> 00:43:25,517
do Q&A on health records or
other kinds of medical data.

1042
00:43:25,517 --> 00:43:26,850
I think that would be important.

1043
00:43:26,850 --> 00:43:29,040
And also extending
the evaluation

1044
00:43:29,040 --> 00:43:31,380
would in terms of scale,
having a diverse panel

1045
00:43:31,380 --> 00:43:33,060
of clinicians vote,
and also in terms

1046
00:43:33,060 --> 00:43:34,500
of the data that you're using.

1047
00:43:34,500 --> 00:43:36,360
Maybe adversarial
modifying the questions

1048
00:43:36,360 --> 00:43:38,620
to include demographic
confounders or something

1049
00:43:38,620 --> 00:43:39,120
like that.

1050
00:43:39,120 --> 00:43:42,120
I think those are all could
be interesting directions.

1051
00:43:42,120 --> 00:43:44,550
I think on the modeling side,
the interesting question

1052
00:43:44,550 --> 00:43:49,470
for me is again, this interplay
between smaller domain

1053
00:43:49,470 --> 00:43:51,870
specific LMs versus
larger purpose LLMs

1054
00:43:51,870 --> 00:43:54,180
and how that's
going to play out.

1055
00:43:54,180 --> 00:43:58,200
There seems to be some evidence
of emergence over here,

1056
00:43:58,200 --> 00:44:00,150
especially with
medical reasoning.

1057
00:44:00,150 --> 00:44:02,625
And so as you can
see, at lower scale,

1058
00:44:02,625 --> 00:44:04,500
sometimes the performance
is not good enough.

1059
00:44:04,500 --> 00:44:06,510
I mean, 50%, that's
a good number

1060
00:44:06,510 --> 00:44:08,370
but that's just not viable.

1061
00:44:08,370 --> 00:44:10,470
But when you get to 80%
or 90%, the products

1062
00:44:10,470 --> 00:44:11,380
really become useful.

1063
00:44:11,380 --> 00:44:15,690
And so we are seeing at bigger
parameter sizes of these models

1064
00:44:15,690 --> 00:44:16,710
but I don't know.

1065
00:44:16,710 --> 00:44:18,840
I think it's still an
open question we're here.

1066
00:44:18,840 --> 00:44:22,270
[INAUDIBLE]

1067
00:44:22,270 --> 00:44:23,967
Yeah.

1068
00:44:23,967 --> 00:44:25,800
The question was, is
hallucination an issue?

1069
00:44:25,800 --> 00:44:29,410
I think it still is but I
believe that you can control

1070
00:44:29,410 --> 00:44:31,330
that fairly well
with instruction

1071
00:44:31,330 --> 00:44:33,370
from tuning, any kind
of feedback data.

1072
00:44:33,370 --> 00:44:36,920
I think it's not
terribly difficult to do.

1073
00:44:36,920 --> 00:44:42,017
And so I think it might have
been overblown generally.

1074
00:44:42,017 --> 00:44:44,350
Especially when you are doing
it in a particular domain,

1075
00:44:44,350 --> 00:44:46,386
I think it's easier to control.

1076
00:44:46,386 --> 00:44:47,372
[INAUDIBLE]

1077
00:44:47,372 --> 00:44:53,950

1078
00:44:53,950 --> 00:44:55,390
What it looks like.

1079
00:44:55,390 --> 00:45:00,190
Say recently, there's been
reporting about [INAUDIBLE]..

1080
00:45:00,190 --> 00:45:06,070

1081
00:45:06,070 --> 00:45:08,500
I'm just curious because
this particular app,

1082
00:45:08,500 --> 00:45:11,410
do you think that's
very, very relevant?

1083
00:45:11,410 --> 00:45:14,743
And [INAUDIBLE] high
bar, the quality of it.

1084
00:45:14,743 --> 00:45:16,410
So I'm just curious
if you're even going

1085
00:45:16,410 --> 00:45:19,630
to speak out about [INAUDIBLE].

1086
00:45:19,630 --> 00:45:20,290
Yeah.

1087
00:45:20,290 --> 00:45:24,010
So the question was, there's
a lot of talk and noise

1088
00:45:24,010 --> 00:45:26,590
around hallucinations
and general purpose LLMs.

1089
00:45:26,590 --> 00:45:28,720
In this particular
application domain,

1090
00:45:28,720 --> 00:45:30,170
it seems particularly relevant.

1091
00:45:30,170 --> 00:45:33,080
And so can you expand on
that a little bit further?

1092
00:45:33,080 --> 00:45:33,580
Sure.

1093
00:45:33,580 --> 00:45:37,720
So what we are seeing is even
with an order of a few hundred

1094
00:45:37,720 --> 00:45:40,960
examples from expert
clinicians, teaching the model

1095
00:45:40,960 --> 00:45:44,320
how to communicate
medical information,

1096
00:45:44,320 --> 00:45:46,900
that is good enough
to get the model

1097
00:45:46,900 --> 00:45:51,220
to maybe stop hallucinating
or at least communicate

1098
00:45:51,220 --> 00:45:52,570
its uncertainty in a better way.

1099
00:45:52,570 --> 00:45:55,240

1100
00:45:55,240 --> 00:45:57,850
At least in this particular
domain or this setting,

1101
00:45:57,850 --> 00:45:59,620
it feels more tractable to us.

1102
00:45:59,620 --> 00:46:01,150
And the reason
I'm saying this is

1103
00:46:01,150 --> 00:46:02,858
we've looked at the
answers qualitatively

1104
00:46:02,858 --> 00:46:05,560
and we are seeing that
the model does not

1105
00:46:05,560 --> 00:46:07,690
tend to generate
super long answers

1106
00:46:07,690 --> 00:46:11,740
or make very
confident predictions.

1107
00:46:11,740 --> 00:46:15,730
But rather, the tone itself
becomes very reserved

1108
00:46:15,730 --> 00:46:18,513
and it starts using
terms like maybe

1109
00:46:18,513 --> 00:46:20,680
this needs to be done further
or something like that

1110
00:46:20,680 --> 00:46:22,400
which communicates uncertainty.

1111
00:46:22,400 --> 00:46:24,223
So how well is that
actually correlated

1112
00:46:24,223 --> 00:46:26,140
with the representation
underlying uncertainty

1113
00:46:26,140 --> 00:46:29,197
that we have, is still I
think an area of research.

1114
00:46:29,197 --> 00:46:31,030
But I think this is
already promising for us

1115
00:46:31,030 --> 00:46:33,160
that it feels controllable
in limited application

1116
00:46:33,160 --> 00:46:34,865
settings like medicine.

1117
00:46:34,865 --> 00:46:36,240
But if you have
a general purpose

1118
00:46:36,240 --> 00:46:37,630
LLM I'm trying to answer
pretty much everything

1119
00:46:37,630 --> 00:46:39,880
about the world, I think
that's a much harder problem.

1120
00:46:39,880 --> 00:46:42,710

1121
00:46:42,710 --> 00:46:47,270
Do you think that would be a
feature of the domain data set?

1122
00:46:47,270 --> 00:46:51,260
In medical situations, doctors
are more reserved perhaps

1123
00:46:51,260 --> 00:46:56,390
and don't have
absolute [INAUDIBLE]

1124
00:46:56,390 --> 00:47:02,670
or do you think it's more
that you just specialize?

1125
00:47:02,670 --> 00:47:05,900
It could be something
else entirely also,

1126
00:47:05,900 --> 00:47:08,580
I'm just curious what you think.

1127
00:47:08,580 --> 00:47:14,570
Yeah, so the question is, do you
think the way how the model is

1128
00:47:14,570 --> 00:47:17,540
performing in this domain is
that a feature of the data

1129
00:47:17,540 --> 00:47:20,925
sets in the medical
domain and typically based

1130
00:47:20,925 --> 00:47:22,050
on how doctors communicate?

1131
00:47:22,050 --> 00:47:23,420
And I think that's true.

1132
00:47:23,420 --> 00:47:24,920
And I think that's
something we need

1133
00:47:24,920 --> 00:47:26,198
to build on and use over here.

1134
00:47:26,198 --> 00:47:27,740
And I think that's
extremely helpful.

1135
00:47:27,740 --> 00:47:30,230
And hopefully this kind of
behavior is general enough

1136
00:47:30,230 --> 00:47:33,080
and can be transmitted
to the model

1137
00:47:33,080 --> 00:47:35,180
even when it's used in
non-medical settings

1138
00:47:35,180 --> 00:47:39,392
to be more reserved
when it's communicating,

1139
00:47:39,392 --> 00:47:41,100
hallucinate less, and
so on and so forth.

1140
00:47:41,100 --> 00:47:43,600
So I believe that that's one
of the opportunities over here,

1141
00:47:43,600 --> 00:47:45,500
to use these benchmarks
come up with methods

1142
00:47:45,500 --> 00:47:48,515
that reduce hallucination,
communicate uncertainty better.

1143
00:47:48,515 --> 00:47:50,390
And then use that as a
bidirectional learning

1144
00:47:50,390 --> 00:47:54,028
opportunity to improve the
general purpose LLM as well.

1145
00:47:54,028 --> 00:47:55,570
So if you have any
further questions,

1146
00:47:55,570 --> 00:47:57,362
I'll come back again
at the end of the talk

1147
00:47:57,362 --> 00:48:00,850
but I want to cover the rest
of the applications as well.

1148
00:48:00,850 --> 00:48:05,400
So the next domain I want
to talk about is proteins.

1149
00:48:05,400 --> 00:48:07,410
And the papers
from now I'm going

1150
00:48:07,410 --> 00:48:11,200
to zip through them a
little bit given time.

1151
00:48:11,200 --> 00:48:14,910
But the first one I want
to talk is this paper

1152
00:48:14,910 --> 00:48:17,520
from a few folks at Google
Research back in 2020,

1153
00:48:17,520 --> 00:48:19,860
called "Masked Language
Modeling for Proteins

1154
00:48:19,860 --> 00:48:24,160
via Linearly Scalable
Long-Context Transformers."

1155
00:48:24,160 --> 00:48:26,170
So the problem here
is that modeling long

1156
00:48:26,170 --> 00:48:29,680
range biological sequences
requires efficient transformer

1157
00:48:29,680 --> 00:48:31,570
architectures.

1158
00:48:31,570 --> 00:48:34,150
And so in this particular
paper, what they introduced

1159
00:48:34,150 --> 00:48:36,430
was this performance
architecture

1160
00:48:36,430 --> 00:48:40,180
which approximates the SOC max
attention kernel via low rank

1161
00:48:40,180 --> 00:48:41,960
decomposition.

1162
00:48:41,960 --> 00:48:45,880
And so this does not incorporate
any sparsity priors say

1163
00:48:45,880 --> 00:48:48,550
like other methods
like the Reformer

1164
00:48:48,550 --> 00:48:51,220
or there are many others.

1165
00:48:51,220 --> 00:48:54,520
And this is good because
sparsity priors may not

1166
00:48:54,520 --> 00:48:57,940
be appropriate for biological
data such as protein

1167
00:48:57,940 --> 00:49:01,210
which required global
interactions to be modeled.

1168
00:49:01,210 --> 00:49:04,660
And then the other thing is this
model, the performance scales

1169
00:49:04,660 --> 00:49:07,750
linearly rather than quadraticly
with the sequence length L.

1170
00:49:07,750 --> 00:49:09,580
And the number of
random features

1171
00:49:09,580 --> 00:49:12,340
that you need to approximate
this SOC max attention kernel

1172
00:49:12,340 --> 00:49:16,180
m, is completely independent
of the input sequence length.

1173
00:49:16,180 --> 00:49:18,463
So just to very
quickly visualize

1174
00:49:18,463 --> 00:49:20,630
the speed ups and the space
complexity improvements.

1175
00:49:20,630 --> 00:49:22,172
What you are having
with this loading

1176
00:49:22,172 --> 00:49:25,060
decomposition is instead of
having fat matrices in your SOC

1177
00:49:25,060 --> 00:49:27,640
max attention kernel, you
now have thinner matrices

1178
00:49:27,640 --> 00:49:31,560
which are determined by the
size of the random features.

1179
00:49:31,560 --> 00:49:33,870
And that basically reduces
your quadratic complexity

1180
00:49:33,870 --> 00:49:35,620
to something that is
more linear in nature

1181
00:49:35,620 --> 00:49:39,550
and also leads to
space improvements.

1182
00:49:39,550 --> 00:49:41,770
Yeah, there are more
theoretical analysis and details

1183
00:49:41,770 --> 00:49:44,750
in the paper, and I would
refer you all back to it.

1184
00:49:44,750 --> 00:49:46,810
But what we see in
terms of results

1185
00:49:46,810 --> 00:49:48,910
when doing protein
language modeling

1186
00:49:48,910 --> 00:49:52,240
is that the accuracy
of this model

1187
00:49:52,240 --> 00:49:55,330
is on par with
transformers while reducing

1188
00:49:55,330 --> 00:49:56,870
computational costs quite a bit.

1189
00:49:56,870 --> 00:49:59,085
And so what this suggests
is that the approximation

1190
00:49:59,085 --> 00:50:01,460
of the SOC max attention kernel
is a tight approximation.

1191
00:50:01,460 --> 00:50:02,780
So that is good.

1192
00:50:02,780 --> 00:50:04,870
And then when you compare
that with other methods

1193
00:50:04,870 --> 00:50:06,880
such as the Reformer
or [INAUDIBLE],,

1194
00:50:06,880 --> 00:50:09,260
the accuracy is much higher
at least on this task.

1195
00:50:09,260 --> 00:50:12,310
So it seems that compared to
other methods that approximate,

1196
00:50:12,310 --> 00:50:14,882
like try to build more
efficient transformers,

1197
00:50:14,882 --> 00:50:16,840
this one is much better
for biological sequence

1198
00:50:16,840 --> 00:50:19,960
data, at least in this setting.

1199
00:50:19,960 --> 00:50:25,080
And finally, if you
look at the attention

1200
00:50:25,080 --> 00:50:28,353
of the amino acid
similarity matrix,

1201
00:50:28,353 --> 00:50:30,270
you can see that the
performa model recognizes

1202
00:50:30,270 --> 00:50:33,780
highly similar amino acid
pairs such as D and E, and F

1203
00:50:33,780 --> 00:50:36,600
and Y over here so that suggests
that the model is learning

1204
00:50:36,600 --> 00:50:39,940
the right set of information
that we really want over here.

1205
00:50:39,940 --> 00:50:43,630
So that was a two-minute
overview of that paper.

1206
00:50:43,630 --> 00:50:46,350
But I want to talk
about another one which

1207
00:50:46,350 --> 00:50:49,360
also I think is really cool.

1208
00:50:49,360 --> 00:50:51,900
So this one is called
ProtNLM, again,

1209
00:50:51,900 --> 00:50:54,000
by a few other folks
at Google Research.

1210
00:50:54,000 --> 00:50:58,710
And what this does is model
based natural language protein

1211
00:50:58,710 --> 00:51:00,150
annotation.

1212
00:51:00,150 --> 00:51:02,310
And why this
problem is important

1213
00:51:02,310 --> 00:51:04,200
is because the
protein information

1214
00:51:04,200 --> 00:51:07,330
is in very high demand.

1215
00:51:07,330 --> 00:51:09,600
So over 50% of all
known proteins that

1216
00:51:09,600 --> 00:51:11,980
have been sequenced, we don't
actually know what they do.

1217
00:51:11,980 --> 00:51:14,340
So it's important that
we able to decipher that

1218
00:51:14,340 --> 00:51:15,600
to some degree at least.

1219
00:51:15,600 --> 00:51:18,618
And then the second thing is
we may want to, for example,

1220
00:51:18,618 --> 00:51:20,910
find protein sequences with
given functions and this is

1221
00:51:20,910 --> 00:51:23,290
particularly important
in the CRISPR domain.

1222
00:51:23,290 --> 00:51:25,560
And so if you can train
bidirectional models that

1223
00:51:25,560 --> 00:51:28,740
can do this, I think that
would be incredibly helpful.

1224
00:51:28,740 --> 00:51:32,230
And the reason I
say this, again,

1225
00:51:32,230 --> 00:51:36,462
is that the unique broad
database that has over--

1226
00:51:36,462 --> 00:51:38,920
that has I think millions of
researchers worldwide using it

1227
00:51:38,920 --> 00:51:39,260
today.

1228
00:51:39,260 --> 00:51:41,710
And so getting this information
populated in that database

1229
00:51:41,710 --> 00:51:45,220
would be incredibly useful and
accelerate a lot of research

1230
00:51:45,220 --> 00:51:47,210
in this space.

1231
00:51:47,210 --> 00:51:50,080
And so the European
Bioinformatics Institute,

1232
00:51:50,080 --> 00:51:53,620
they have curated this
pretext data about proteins.

1233
00:51:53,620 --> 00:51:56,230
And so basically, you can
use this protein record

1234
00:51:56,230 --> 00:51:58,250
to train these models.

1235
00:51:58,250 --> 00:52:00,700
And so what you
want to do is you

1236
00:52:00,700 --> 00:52:03,760
want to maybe learn to directly
map from amino acid sequences

1237
00:52:03,760 --> 00:52:06,490
to natural language
descriptions of them.

1238
00:52:06,490 --> 00:52:08,950
And this problem is not
too different from an image

1239
00:52:08,950 --> 00:52:13,240
captioning problem where instead
of having a sequence of pixels,

1240
00:52:13,240 --> 00:52:15,630
I don't know if sequence is
right but if you have pixels,

1241
00:52:15,630 --> 00:52:17,380
instead you have a
sequence of amino acids

1242
00:52:17,380 --> 00:52:20,440
and they can range a
number from 2 to 40k.

1243
00:52:20,440 --> 00:52:22,030
And then what you
want to generate out

1244
00:52:22,030 --> 00:52:26,253
is a description of the protein.

1245
00:52:26,253 --> 00:52:27,670
And in this paper,
the way they do

1246
00:52:27,670 --> 00:52:32,170
this is they train a T5 model
on protein sequence annotation

1247
00:52:32,170 --> 00:52:32,780
tasks.

1248
00:52:32,780 --> 00:52:35,720
So the tasks are set up in
a bunch of different ways.

1249
00:52:35,720 --> 00:52:38,860
And the supervised
data comes from a bunch

1250
00:52:38,860 --> 00:52:41,720
of different sources in the
protein record that they have.

1251
00:52:41,720 --> 00:52:44,830
And this model is
an encoded T5 model.

1252
00:52:44,830 --> 00:52:46,210
So it's a very cool application.

1253
00:52:46,210 --> 00:52:49,930
And the results are that out
of the 56 million proteins

1254
00:52:49,930 --> 00:52:52,240
in that UniProt database
that were previously

1255
00:52:52,240 --> 00:52:54,910
uncharacterized,
49 million of them

1256
00:52:54,910 --> 00:52:56,800
now have associated
textual descriptions.

1257
00:52:56,800 --> 00:53:00,377
So we now have a
handle on what they do.

1258
00:53:00,377 --> 00:53:01,460
And so that's really cool.

1259
00:53:01,460 --> 00:53:03,220
And then the other one I think
which is probably even more

1260
00:53:03,220 --> 00:53:05,230
interesting is now
you can run queries

1261
00:53:05,230 --> 00:53:08,857
like find me a smaller version
of this CRISPR Cas9 protein

1262
00:53:08,857 --> 00:53:10,690
so that it can target
certain tissues better

1263
00:53:10,690 --> 00:53:12,980
and now the model can
come back with sequences.

1264
00:53:12,980 --> 00:53:15,190
And so I think this is going
to be incredibly useful

1265
00:53:15,190 --> 00:53:17,853
and going to accelerate a lot
of research in this space.

1266
00:53:17,853 --> 00:53:19,270
Already there's a
lot of momentum.

1267
00:53:19,270 --> 00:53:21,228
I think these models are
going to further help.

1268
00:53:21,228 --> 00:53:24,770

1269
00:53:24,770 --> 00:53:25,790
So that was on proteins.

1270
00:53:25,790 --> 00:53:27,915
The last class of applications
that I want to cover

1271
00:53:27,915 --> 00:53:30,770
is on the genomics side.

1272
00:53:30,770 --> 00:53:34,670
Again, the first paper over
here was some work last year

1273
00:53:34,670 --> 00:53:37,410
from our genomics team
at Health AI at Google,

1274
00:53:37,410 --> 00:53:40,940
which is building gap
aware sequence transformers

1275
00:53:40,940 --> 00:53:43,110
for sequence collection.

1276
00:53:43,110 --> 00:53:45,200
So this module is
called DeepConsensus.

1277
00:53:45,200 --> 00:53:49,110
And so what role does this model
play and why does it matter?

1278
00:53:49,110 --> 00:53:53,720
So if you look at the sequencing
data lifecycle, what you do

1279
00:53:53,720 --> 00:53:56,690
is you go from
basically atoms to bits.

1280
00:53:56,690 --> 00:53:58,520
And so you have this
physical specimen that

1281
00:53:58,520 --> 00:54:01,160
hopefully has some DNA in it.

1282
00:54:01,160 --> 00:54:03,710
And you put it
through a sequencing

1283
00:54:03,710 --> 00:54:07,430
machine such as PacBio and that
comes out with the raw data.

1284
00:54:07,430 --> 00:54:11,480
And that raw data gets
mapped to a reference genome.

1285
00:54:11,480 --> 00:54:13,340
And then sometimes
there might be

1286
00:54:13,340 --> 00:54:15,530
DIFS between an individual
and the reference genome

1287
00:54:15,530 --> 00:54:17,480
and that can be corrected
through this model called deep

1288
00:54:17,480 --> 00:54:19,813
variant that was introduced
by our team a few years back

1289
00:54:19,813 --> 00:54:20,870
and that's open source.

1290
00:54:20,870 --> 00:54:23,462
And then once you
have this sequence,

1291
00:54:23,462 --> 00:54:25,670
you can then use it for a
bunch of different analysis

1292
00:54:25,670 --> 00:54:32,350
such as ancestry or just
basic biomedical research.

1293
00:54:32,350 --> 00:54:34,680
So where deep variant
fits in is it's

1294
00:54:34,680 --> 00:54:37,470
actually makes the raw
DNA reads that comes out

1295
00:54:37,470 --> 00:54:39,300
from the PacBio sequencer.

1296
00:54:39,300 --> 00:54:42,010
It tries to make
it more accurate.

1297
00:54:42,010 --> 00:54:45,600
And so how the PacBio
sequencer actually works

1298
00:54:45,600 --> 00:54:51,180
is it uses this clear consensus
sequencing algorithm where

1299
00:54:51,180 --> 00:54:54,310
the DNA molecule is
read several times,

1300
00:54:54,310 --> 00:54:56,250
and it produces multiple
different subreads.

1301
00:54:56,250 --> 00:55:00,120
And these subreads, they
do contain some errors

1302
00:55:00,120 --> 00:55:02,410
and so they are finally
assembled together.

1303
00:55:02,410 --> 00:55:04,740
And so what deep
variant tries to do

1304
00:55:04,740 --> 00:55:08,130
is it tries to improve on the
errors over here basically that

1305
00:55:08,130 --> 00:55:10,560
comes out from just this
circular consensus sequencing

1306
00:55:10,560 --> 00:55:12,920
algorithm.

1307
00:55:12,920 --> 00:55:14,832
And so how does this model work?

1308
00:55:14,832 --> 00:55:16,790
So as I said, the basic
task for deep consensus

1309
00:55:16,790 --> 00:55:20,240
is to use the CCS
data and the subreads

1310
00:55:20,240 --> 00:55:23,302
associated with them to
generate a corrected sequence.

1311
00:55:23,302 --> 00:55:25,760
And so in this example, when
we run through the model, what

1312
00:55:25,760 --> 00:55:29,150
we see is that while the CCS
identity was at like 95.7%,

1313
00:55:29,150 --> 00:55:32,250
the DeepConsensus prediction
identity was 100%.

1314
00:55:32,250 --> 00:55:33,770
So it's a fairly
simple task where

1315
00:55:33,770 --> 00:55:35,570
you're trying to
reduce errors that

1316
00:55:35,570 --> 00:55:39,120
come out from the PacBio
with the CCS algorithm.

1317
00:55:39,120 --> 00:55:41,990
And so the very natural question
is, where do these labels

1318
00:55:41,990 --> 00:55:43,560
come from?

1319
00:55:43,560 --> 00:55:47,210
So each CCS sequence
that you have, that is

1320
00:55:47,210 --> 00:55:49,890
aligned to a high
quality assembly.

1321
00:55:49,890 --> 00:55:51,740
And this high
quality assembly is

1322
00:55:51,740 --> 00:55:56,790
created by having many CCS
reads stitched together.

1323
00:55:56,790 --> 00:55:59,100
And so that ends up
having fewer errors.

1324
00:55:59,100 --> 00:56:03,590
And so you can then try to
use that high quality stitched

1325
00:56:03,590 --> 00:56:07,940
assembly and map that back to
the CCS trade for a given block

1326
00:56:07,940 --> 00:56:09,120
and use that as that label.

1327
00:56:09,120 --> 00:56:13,087
So that results in more
stronger ground truth

1328
00:56:13,087 --> 00:56:15,170
and you can use that to
train the model to improve

1329
00:56:15,170 --> 00:56:16,440
the accuracy further.

1330
00:56:16,440 --> 00:56:18,770
And so this is what the
model is trained on.

1331
00:56:18,770 --> 00:56:20,940
And so the model
looks like this.

1332
00:56:20,940 --> 00:56:23,060
It's a transformer architecture.

1333
00:56:23,060 --> 00:56:27,240
It takes these subreads
and this CCS read as well.

1334
00:56:27,240 --> 00:56:29,660
And it has a bunch of
additional context features

1335
00:56:29,660 --> 00:56:31,880
that come in from
the sequence itself,

1336
00:56:31,880 --> 00:56:34,890
the instrument sequencing
instrument as well.

1337
00:56:34,890 --> 00:56:37,280
And these are all fed into
the transformer model.

1338
00:56:37,280 --> 00:56:39,892
It produces a polished
segment and these segments

1339
00:56:39,892 --> 00:56:42,350
are then stitched together to
produce the final polish read

1340
00:56:42,350 --> 00:56:45,060
over here.

1341
00:56:45,060 --> 00:56:46,560
One thing I will
point out over here

1342
00:56:46,560 --> 00:56:48,840
is that in order to
train this model,

1343
00:56:48,840 --> 00:56:50,790
you can't use a
cross entropy loss.

1344
00:56:50,790 --> 00:56:55,990
And this is because
if you insert--

1345
00:56:55,990 --> 00:56:58,190
you often have insertions
in DNA sequences.

1346
00:56:58,190 --> 00:57:01,330
And so that can when you use a
cross entropy loss really throw

1347
00:57:01,330 --> 00:57:02,050
off the model.

1348
00:57:02,050 --> 00:57:04,270
Even like a single error
as you can see over here,

1349
00:57:04,270 --> 00:57:06,400
can propagate
throughout the sequence

1350
00:57:06,400 --> 00:57:07,923
and make it really worse.

1351
00:57:07,923 --> 00:57:09,340
So what you need
is a special kind

1352
00:57:09,340 --> 00:57:12,370
of alignment loss
based on distance

1353
00:57:12,370 --> 00:57:15,830
that can really capture this
error much, much better.

1354
00:57:15,830 --> 00:57:18,875
And so making this
alignment last work on TPUs

1355
00:57:18,875 --> 00:57:20,500
and making it
differentiable is I think

1356
00:57:20,500 --> 00:57:21,530
the real meat of this paper.

1357
00:57:21,530 --> 00:57:23,080
And so again, go
back to the paper

1358
00:57:23,080 --> 00:57:24,872
if you're interested
in that kind of topic.

1359
00:57:24,872 --> 00:57:26,560
I think that's really cool.

1360
00:57:26,560 --> 00:57:30,220
But at a very high level, how
well does this model work?

1361
00:57:30,220 --> 00:57:32,700
So if you look at the final
output, you have the read name.

1362
00:57:32,700 --> 00:57:34,448
You have the base
predictions and also

1363
00:57:34,448 --> 00:57:36,240
the predicted quality,
which can be thought

1364
00:57:36,240 --> 00:57:37,282
of as a confidence score.

1365
00:57:37,282 --> 00:57:39,837
And these base predictions
are often quite long

1366
00:57:39,837 --> 00:57:41,670
and so you can see that
continuous offscreen

1367
00:57:41,670 --> 00:57:45,140
because it's 10k to 20k
cases long over here.

1368
00:57:45,140 --> 00:57:46,560
And when you look
at the quality,

1369
00:57:46,560 --> 00:57:50,920
it improved quite a bit over
the [INAUDIBLE] CSS algorithm

1370
00:57:50,920 --> 00:57:51,420
over here.

1371
00:57:51,420 --> 00:57:54,910
The per read accuracy over
here improves quite a bit.

1372
00:57:54,910 --> 00:57:58,350
And so you may ask like,
what is the real world

1373
00:57:58,350 --> 00:58:01,200
impact of this kind of model?

1374
00:58:01,200 --> 00:58:03,212
So the answer is
this model is already

1375
00:58:03,212 --> 00:58:04,420
being used in the real world.

1376
00:58:04,420 --> 00:58:06,300
So at Stanford in
the genomics team

1377
00:58:06,300 --> 00:58:07,950
by Dr. Ashley and
a few others, there

1378
00:58:07,950 --> 00:58:10,857
was this recent ultra
rapid nanopore genome

1379
00:58:10,857 --> 00:58:13,440
sequencing paper where they set
a world record for the fastest

1380
00:58:13,440 --> 00:58:14,370
genome sequencing.

1381
00:58:14,370 --> 00:58:16,650
And this deep consensus
transform architecture

1382
00:58:16,650 --> 00:58:18,640
was used in that
assembly sequence.

1383
00:58:18,640 --> 00:58:20,610
And so in this
particular study, they

1384
00:58:20,610 --> 00:58:23,640
were able to very quickly
diagnose that Matthew over here

1385
00:58:23,640 --> 00:58:26,920
had a heart condition
due to genetic reasons.

1386
00:58:26,920 --> 00:58:28,740
And so they were very
quickly able to put

1387
00:58:28,740 --> 00:58:31,705
Matthew on the patient's
donors list over here.

1388
00:58:31,705 --> 00:58:33,330
So that's the kind
of real world impact

1389
00:58:33,330 --> 00:58:36,360
we can have with these
biomedical transfer models

1390
00:58:36,360 --> 00:58:37,560
and AI systems in general.

1391
00:58:37,560 --> 00:58:40,220

1392
00:58:40,220 --> 00:58:44,830
And very quickly, the last
paper that I want to talk

1393
00:58:44,830 --> 00:58:48,580
about is this paper from
DeepMind on Effective Gene

1394
00:58:48,580 --> 00:58:51,820
Expression Prediction from
Sequences By Integrating Long

1395
00:58:51,820 --> 00:58:53,110
Range Interactions."

1396
00:58:53,110 --> 00:58:56,440
This was published
in Nature Methods.

1397
00:58:56,440 --> 00:58:58,600
And the motivation for
this work is again,

1398
00:58:58,600 --> 00:59:00,310
that since the Human
Genome Project,

1399
00:59:00,310 --> 00:59:02,950
there have been thousands of
genome wide association study

1400
00:59:02,950 --> 00:59:08,500
hits where the goal is
to map genetic variants

1401
00:59:08,500 --> 00:59:10,870
to different kind of
disease phenotypes.

1402
00:59:10,870 --> 00:59:14,537
But a lot of this involves
experimentation and real world

1403
00:59:14,537 --> 00:59:16,370
experimentation patient
takes a lot of time.

1404
00:59:16,370 --> 00:59:18,700
So if you can do that with
machine learning models,

1405
00:59:18,700 --> 00:59:20,110
that's really great.

1406
00:59:20,110 --> 00:59:23,480
And so that's what they set
out to do in this paper.

1407
00:59:23,480 --> 00:59:26,230
And so if you look
at the gene itself,

1408
00:59:26,230 --> 00:59:29,230
there are 10% of
the gene are going

1409
00:59:29,230 --> 00:59:32,260
to be like coding variants
and these influence protein

1410
00:59:32,260 --> 00:59:33,550
function.

1411
00:59:33,550 --> 00:59:35,350
And then the way they
can cause diseases

1412
00:59:35,350 --> 00:59:38,770
is by disrupting the structure
of proteins that are generated

1413
00:59:38,770 --> 00:59:42,342
or by affecting the
protein-protein interactions.

1414
00:59:42,342 --> 00:59:44,050
The good part about
these coding variants

1415
00:59:44,050 --> 00:59:45,633
are they tend to be
closer to the gene

1416
00:59:45,633 --> 00:59:47,890
and so they're
easier to interpret.

1417
00:59:47,890 --> 00:59:50,200
On the other hand,
the 90% of the gene

1418
00:59:50,200 --> 00:59:52,090
is like non-coding variants.

1419
00:59:52,090 --> 00:59:56,060
And the way they work is they
influence protein expression.

1420
00:59:56,060 --> 01:00:00,410
So they are more like
regulatory sequences.

1421
01:00:00,410 --> 01:00:03,250
And so the way they
can lead to diseases

1422
01:00:03,250 --> 01:00:05,980
if they have any
variance is by disrupting

1423
01:00:05,980 --> 01:00:08,920
the transcription of proteins.

1424
01:00:08,920 --> 01:00:12,160
And given that these non-coding
variants can be very, very far

1425
01:00:12,160 --> 01:00:14,710
away from the gene and
the coding variants,

1426
01:00:14,710 --> 01:00:16,640
it's very difficult
to interpret them.

1427
01:00:16,640 --> 01:00:18,640
And so the question is,
can we try and transform

1428
01:00:18,640 --> 01:00:20,200
models that can
predict the influence

1429
01:00:20,200 --> 01:00:21,910
of these non-coding variants?

1430
01:00:21,910 --> 01:00:23,900
And so that is the
task over here.

1431
01:00:23,900 --> 01:00:27,010
And so this is a
visualization again.

1432
01:00:27,010 --> 01:00:31,740
So the paper again
looks at the--

1433
01:00:31,740 --> 01:00:33,460
it focuses on
transcription, which

1434
01:00:33,460 --> 01:00:36,940
is the first step in terms
of converting DNA into RNA.

1435
01:00:36,940 --> 01:00:40,480
And the way this is done is
you have RNA polymerase, which

1436
01:00:40,480 --> 01:00:43,270
gets recruited at the
beginning of the gene

1437
01:00:43,270 --> 01:00:46,180
by these proteins called
transcription factors.

1438
01:00:46,180 --> 01:00:48,310
And these transcription
factors have a binding site

1439
01:00:48,310 --> 01:00:50,320
which correspond to
these promoters which

1440
01:00:50,320 --> 01:00:51,490
are quite close to the gene.

1441
01:00:51,490 --> 01:00:53,500
But then you also
have these enhancers

1442
01:00:53,500 --> 01:00:57,160
which can be very, very far away
from these promoters in terms

1443
01:00:57,160 --> 01:01:01,870
of the linear space also
influencing this transcription.

1444
01:01:01,870 --> 01:01:04,965
And you may ask, how can such--

1445
01:01:04,965 --> 01:01:07,090
how can these enhancers
influence the activity over

1446
01:01:07,090 --> 01:01:07,450
here?

1447
01:01:07,450 --> 01:01:09,200
This is because while
they may be far away

1448
01:01:09,200 --> 01:01:12,320
in the linear space,
when the sequence folds

1449
01:01:12,320 --> 01:01:14,287
and in the 3D structure,
they will end up

1450
01:01:14,287 --> 01:01:15,620
being quite close to each other.

1451
01:01:15,620 --> 01:01:17,745
And so they can completely
affect the transcription

1452
01:01:17,745 --> 01:01:18,850
process over here.

1453
01:01:18,850 --> 01:01:20,710
So it's a very
high level overview

1454
01:01:20,710 --> 01:01:23,920
of what's happening over here
and in terms of the biology.

1455
01:01:23,920 --> 01:01:25,690
And so the question
is if there are

1456
01:01:25,690 --> 01:01:28,750
any variants in these
non-coding variants

1457
01:01:28,750 --> 01:01:30,790
and in these enhancers.

1458
01:01:30,790 --> 01:01:33,190
They may disrupt the
transcription factor binding

1459
01:01:33,190 --> 01:01:35,830
and this can in turn
lead to no proteins

1460
01:01:35,830 --> 01:01:37,330
and then finally
to diseases, right?

1461
01:01:37,330 --> 01:01:39,040
So we want to be
able to predict that

1462
01:01:39,040 --> 01:01:42,130
based on the DNA sequences
that have been generated.

1463
01:01:42,130 --> 01:01:45,320
So the problem is
quite straightforward.

1464
01:01:45,320 --> 01:01:47,280
It's a supervised
learning problem.

1465
01:01:47,280 --> 01:01:49,230
The setup is predict
experimental data

1466
01:01:49,230 --> 01:01:51,160
from these DNA sequences.

1467
01:01:51,160 --> 01:01:53,397
And this can take
many different forms.

1468
01:01:53,397 --> 01:01:55,230
The primary one is gene
expression over here

1469
01:01:55,230 --> 01:01:56,860
but then there are
also other tasks,

1470
01:01:56,860 --> 01:01:59,675
such as DNA accessibility,
histone modifications,

1471
01:01:59,675 --> 01:02:01,050
and transcription
factor binding,

1472
01:02:01,050 --> 01:02:04,120
and so on and so forth.

1473
01:02:04,120 --> 01:02:06,570
So as you can imagine,
the baseline model

1474
01:02:06,570 --> 01:02:10,650
for this task for many
years was the CNN model.

1475
01:02:10,650 --> 01:02:12,930
And as you start to get a
different set of layers,

1476
01:02:12,930 --> 01:02:14,430
you can increase
the receptive field

1477
01:02:14,430 --> 01:02:16,270
but there's a limit to that.

1478
01:02:16,270 --> 01:02:18,195
So in this work,
what they showed was

1479
01:02:18,195 --> 01:02:21,750
you can use transformers
instead and do

1480
01:02:21,750 --> 01:02:25,045
better modeling of these
long range interactions.

1481
01:02:25,045 --> 01:02:26,670
So the final model
is called "Enformer"

1482
01:02:26,670 --> 01:02:30,730
which is a combination of
this enhancer and transformer.

1483
01:02:30,730 --> 01:02:32,430
And so if you look
at the model itself,

1484
01:02:32,430 --> 01:02:34,300
it has a few CNN layers
at the beginning,

1485
01:02:34,300 --> 01:02:36,175
but then it has a bunch
of transformer blocks

1486
01:02:36,175 --> 01:02:37,560
that are stacked together.

1487
01:02:37,560 --> 01:02:41,790
And the input is
200kb DNA sequences

1488
01:02:41,790 --> 01:02:43,472
and there are
approximately 30 examples

1489
01:02:43,472 --> 01:02:44,430
that have been trained.

1490
01:02:44,430 --> 01:02:47,700
And the output is like genomic
tracks of this RNA expression

1491
01:02:47,700 --> 01:02:48,385
with.

1492
01:02:48,385 --> 01:02:50,760
And they have organism specific
so one for humans and one

1493
01:02:50,760 --> 01:02:53,910
for mouse.

1494
01:02:53,910 --> 01:02:57,245
And finally, one key detail is
that relative position encoding

1495
01:02:57,245 --> 01:02:59,370
that were used in this
model but actually very key.

1496
01:02:59,370 --> 01:03:01,530
And these relative position
encoding for modeling,

1497
01:03:01,530 --> 01:03:04,090
this power of interactions.

1498
01:03:04,090 --> 01:03:06,913
And as a result of using this
relative position encoding

1499
01:03:06,913 --> 01:03:08,580
with the transformer
block architecture,

1500
01:03:08,580 --> 01:03:12,120
they were now able to model
interactions over 100 base

1501
01:03:12,120 --> 01:03:13,230
pairs.

1502
01:03:13,230 --> 01:03:16,230
And so you see that in
the results over here.

1503
01:03:16,230 --> 01:03:18,790
So you have the
experimental data in green.

1504
01:03:18,790 --> 01:03:20,820
And you can see the
CNN baseline over here.

1505
01:03:20,820 --> 01:03:25,080
And you see that as
soon as you go far away,

1506
01:03:25,080 --> 01:03:29,130
you see that the CNN model
is no longer able to capture

1507
01:03:29,130 --> 01:03:31,320
these gene expressions.

1508
01:03:31,320 --> 01:03:33,985
But you can see that the
enhancer model is now

1509
01:03:33,985 --> 01:03:34,860
able to pick them up.

